&#8220;He knows how this works,&#8221; Rich stated. &#8220;He knows who the audience is.&#8221;

As previously reported by Billboard , Jelly Roll served as a guest host on Jimmy Kimmel Live! last week. He addressed the reaction to jokes he made about President Donald Trump and the public speculation surrounding his political beliefs. The &#8220;Need a Favor&#8221; singer said that while he leans left on certain issues, he considers himself &#8220;pretty down the middle&#8221; politically.

For Rich, the fact that Jelly Roll spoke openly despite the potential backlash was evidence that the singer meant what he said.

&#8220;When you make statements that are that hardcore and, to a large extent, counteractive to the presentation you&#8217;ve been making, you do understand in country music that half or more of your audience may disown you,&#8221; Rich said, adding that some listeners could &#8220;stop listening&#8221; altogether.

Rich pointed to authenticity as a trait of country music, invoking artists including Johnny Cash, Merle Haggard, Loretta Lynn and Dolly Parton as performers whose perspectives became part of their identities. He also connected Jelly Roll&#8217;s decision to his own experiences speaking publicly about politics, which Rich said had resulted in professional consequences for him.

Still, Rich said Jelly Roll&#8217;s situation was distinct because some fans may have been caught off guard by what the singer had to say.

&#8220;Jelly Roll knew the same thing concerning his audience,&#8221; Rich said. &#8220;Yet, he did it anyway. So to me, what that says is this guy is really who he says he is.&#8221;

Jelly Roll himself pushed back against the idea that his stint behind Kimmel&#8217;s desk should define him politically. &#8220;Hosting Jimmy Kimmel&#8217;s show doesn&#8217;t mean I have all of Jimmy Kimmel&#8217;s politics,&#8221; he said during his final night as guest host, later emphasizing his Christian faith, country background and life as a farmer and bow hunter.

He also spoke about the irony of how quickly public perceptions had changed. &#8220;Before this week everybody thought I was MAGA,&#8221; Jelly Roll said. &#8220;And now they think I&#8217;m some sort of liberal snowflake.&#8221;

Rich suggested that the backlash may have less to do with Jelly Roll expressing a specific set of beliefs than the disconnect between those beliefs and what some listeners thought they already knew about him.

&#8220;He should have been talking that way from the very beginning,&#8221; Rich said. &#8220;and he probably wouldn&#8217;t have this problem.&#8221;

